WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.043
RMF is closely associated with NIST guidance.

2
00:00:03.043 --> 00:00:14.079
NIST Special Publication 800-37 describes the Risk Management Framework process and how organizations can apply it to information systems and organizations.

3
00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:22.500
NIST Special Publication 800-53 is commonly associated with the security and privacy controls used in RMF.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:32.906
It provides a catalog of controls that organizations can select and tailor based on system impact, risk context, mission needs, and organizational requirements.

5
00:00:32.906 --> 00:00:45.987
Other NIST publications and organizational policies may support categorization, assessment, continuous monitoring, privacy risk management, system security planning, and control implementation.

6
00:00:45.987 --> 00:00:49.539
RMF often sits within a broader governance ecosystem.

7
00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:56.025
In practice, NIST guidance helps teams move from abstract risk concerns to concrete control decisions.

8
00:00:56.025 --> 00:01:06.341
Instead of saying a system should be secure, RMF helps teams define which controls are relevant, how they will be implemented, and how evidence will show that they are working.

9
00:01:06.341 --> 00:01:09.644
The control catalog is not meant to be copied blindly.

10
00:01:09.644 --> 00:01:18.903
Organizations select and tailor controls based on the system, data, environment, threats, mission, law, policy, and risk tolerance.

11
00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:24.730
This tailoring is one reason RMF is a risk management framework rather than only a checklist.

12
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.624
NIST also emphasizes ongoing risk management.

13
00:01:27.624 --> 00:01:39.787
The framework includes preparation, categorization, selection, implementation, assessment, authorization, and continuous monitoring because systems and environments change over time.

14
00:01:39.787 --> 00:01:44.706
For learners, the important point is that RMF gives structure to risk management.

15
00:01:44.706 --> 00:01:53.756
NIST publications provide the common vocabulary and process model, while each organization applies that model to its own systems and responsibilities.

16
00:01:53.756 --> 00:01:57.218
This common vocabulary matters in large organizations.

17
00:01:57.218 --> 00:02:05.549
It allows security, privacy, engineering, operations, and leadership teams to discuss controls and risk with less ambiguity.
